    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d263">

                <pb facs="d263-1" n="1" xml:id="d263-1"/>

                <head>263. Memorandum of Conversation<anchor n="1" xml:id="d263-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d263-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Mexico City</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        type="conversation-or-meeting-date" when="1976-12-01">December 1,
                        1976</date>
                </dateline>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>PARTICIPANTS:</label>
                    <item> U.S. Side</item>
                    <item>The Secretary</item>
                    <item>Mr. William Rogers, Under Secretary for Economic Affairs</item>
                    <item>Colombia Side</item>
                    <item>Foreign Minister Indalecio Lievano Aguirre</item>
                </list>
                <p>The Secretary met over lunch with Foreign Minister Lievano. Lievano stressed
                    human rights and touched on Panama and economic relations as well.</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#underline">Human Rights</hi>: This issue was at the top of
                    Lievano’s agenda. His suggestion was blunt: do not hang U.S. policy in the
                    hemisphere around the human rights issue. To make human rights performance the
                    test of our relationship with the nations of the Americas would chill our ties
                    with a goodly number who happen not to share our own democratic preferences just
                    now, would create no end of difficulties for the few other democracies, and
                    would probably not do much for human rights.</p>
                <p>[Omitted here is material unrelated to human rights policy.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d263-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d263-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Foreign Policy Files, P820118–1945.
                    Secret; Sensitive; Nodis.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d263-f2" n="2" xml:id="d263-f2-content">
                <p>Colombian Foreign Minister Lievano presented his views to Kissinger and Rogers
                    concerning incorporating human rights issues into U.S.–Latin American
                    relations.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
